  + - `commands_memory.rs` — `/remember`, `/memories`, `/forget` REPL handlers on top of `src/memory.rs`. Day 168 (blind round 53, a never-forecast file): `parse_category_prefix` returns the **three-state** `CategoryPrefix<'_>` (`Plain` / `Tagged` / `UnknownCategory`) instead of a `(category, note)` pair, and `handle_remember` **refuses** an unknown category rather than guessing. Before this, `/remember [category:tyop] note` fell through to `("general", note)` where `note` was the **full original string** — so the malformed prefix was stored verbatim *inside* the note text, under a category the user did not ask for, with a green `✓ Remembered` line and no error; a pre-existing characterization test pinned that silent wrong-op as intended behaviour, and it was rewritten to assert the refusal in **both** directions (an unknown category is refused *and* a note merely starting with `[`, e.g. `[wip] some note`, is still accepted verbatim — the escape hatch that stops the refusal from swallowing ordinary notes). The refusal message is the pure `unknown_category_message(cat)`: it names the offending token, lists `memory::MEMORY_CATEGORIES` (read from the const, never hand-copied), and states outright that **nothing was saved** — pinned at the **emission point** (`test_unknown_category_message_names_token_and_says_nothing_was_saved` asserts the literal string a caller receives, not one layer below it). `/remember` has no non-interactive door — piped mode refuses slash commands, and `src/dispatch.rs:251` is the sole route — so this is a REPL-surface change only. **Known and unfixed, filed as #769:** the `[category:TYPE]` grammar is now *enforced* by the parser but appears on no discoverability surface — `/help remember` (`help_data.rs:1051-1060`) and the completion arg hint (`commands.rs:290`) both still say `/remember <note>`, and the only place the grammar and its five values are shown is `handle_remember`'s own no-argument usage text. The fix reaches two other files (and `help_data.rs` carries the drift-guard suite), so it exceeded round 53's ≤25-line in-file cap; the honest fix ties the documented list to `MEMORY_CATEGORIES` rather than adding a fourth hand-copy. **Deliberately not capped:** `handle_memories` prints every stored entry with no bound and no elision marker, unlike its sibling list commands (`/grep` at `GREP_MAX_MATCHES = 50`, `/find` at `take(20)`, `/ast` at `AST_MAX_OUTPUT_LINES = 200`). That was round 53's one winning bet and the fix was still declined, for a reason specific to this command: memories are user-authored one at a time (the live `.yoyo/memory.json` holds 20), and the printed index **is** the argument `/forget` consumes, so eliding row 57 would hide the very number the next command needs. Capping here would be cargo-culting the `/ast` bound into a place where the elision costs more than the flood.
